WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.085 --> 00:00:08.305
Farming in America

00:00:08.305 --> 00:00:10.803
could not exist without John Deere.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.890
I don't care where you are,

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.370
in the rural parts of
America, there's farmland,

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.053
and you're inevitably going
to see green tractors.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.720
Specifically in the Midwest

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.970
where people who've had Deere
machines for generations

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.410
say that their family bleed green,

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.043
it's really to that extent.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.330
But John Deere's tractors

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.180
aren't the only thing bleeding green.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.420
John Deere is on pace

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.440
to have its most profitable year ever.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.040
John Deere is forecast

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.040
to make about $7.7 billion
in their fiscal year, 2021.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.120
That would be a record.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.850
But while John Deere shareholders

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.170
and executives could not be happier,

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.283
John Deere has been in
a battle on two fronts.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.410
Between a massive union
strike from upset workers

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.030
and a legal battle with farmers

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.080
concerning equipment repairs
called right to repair,

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.370
John Deere must convince their
customers and their employees

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.840
that they care about
more than just profits.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.323
This is The Breakdown.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:30.240
There's a saying that you'll hear

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.267
either Deere say or other people say,

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:35.500
"If farmers are profiting,
Deere's profiting."

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.370
And Deere wants to do everything they can

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:41.133
to make those farmers
a lot more profitable.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.180
Since the pandemic hit in 2020,

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.430
and millions of Americans
sheltered in place

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:50.020
buying nothing but groceries,
both John Deere and farmers

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:53.370
became even more essential
in feeding America

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:56.950
and both profited a
great deal as a result.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.320
Because the machines that they make

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.650
are essential to an industry

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:04.400
that has to provide an
essential product for consumers,

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.580
not only in the United
States, but across the globe.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.490
John Deere's profits during the pandemic

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:10.760
are eye-popping,

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.790
and that wealth has
spread out to executives

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:17.723
like CEO, John May, who brought
in $15,6 million in 2020,

00:02:17.723 --> 00:02:20.770
a 160% raise.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:22.670
And according to our Bloomberg data,

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:26.100
he's gonna make about 30 million
for the 2021 fiscal year.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.480
So you're talking about
almost $10 million more

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.680
for the CEO earnings.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.640
Shareholders also saw
their slice of the pie

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.493
in part because of John
Deere stock buybacks.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.100
Buybacks are a piece of financial
engineering by a company

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.320
to repurchase shares of its own stock

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.680
to reduce the number of
shares in the market,

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.060
which in-turn creases the ownership stake

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.370
for the shareholders.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.540
The stock buybacks for
them have been significant.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.770
So in the past 12 months,

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:56.900
they bought back $1.9 billion in stock,

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.340
and they bought back 716 million alone

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.843
in their fiscal third quarter of 2021.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:05.633
One group who thought

00:03:05.633 --> 00:03:07.460
they were not sharing in that wealth,

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.313
John Deere factory workers.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.680
So the optics

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.840
are obviously looking bad for the company

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.870
now that a labor strike has broken out

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.920
among part of their workforce.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.170
During this boom time,

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.540
Deere offered their
workers 5% pay increase,

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.180
that's when over 10,000
United Auto Workers

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:32.190
who worked for John Deere went on strike.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.977
I had one person in particular say,

00:03:34.977 --> 00:03:39.977
"Our CEO is about to make $10
million more for this year

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:42.330
than he did last year.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.580
The company is seeing
earnings go to records.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.070
The stock price is surging,

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:52.350
and yet we're the group who
during the past recession

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:56.140
and past down economic cycles
in the farming industry,

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.710
we took cuts."

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.130
After the union turned down two contracts,

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:02.450
John Deere was finally
able to meet their demands

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:03.520
in late November,

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.920
which doubled raises and improved pensions

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.500
for union members.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.840
Those employees that are
represented by the UAW

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.980
are immensely valuable employees to us.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:14.160
We're super proud of where
the contract ended up.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.810
It's groundbreaking in a lot of ways,

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.680
both from near-term wage increases,

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.780
as well as the retirement
medical benefits that it offers.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.840
It's super helpful to
understand how the work is done

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:25.800
in the factories,

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.200
and what the opportunities
are to make improvements

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.970
for whatever the operation happens to be

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.833
to make the lives of those that
are doing that work better.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:37.560
So while John Deere workers

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.500
fought and won their
battle to see the profits

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.230
the company has brought in
over the past two years,

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:46.030
farmers are still in
their own fight with Deere

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:48.380
when it comes to repairing
their own equipment.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:03.510
So a lot of farms our size
use a variety of technology.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.880
We use old technology,
we use new technology.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.220
My name is Tom Brandt,

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.650
and you are currently
in Plymouth, Nebraska.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:14.600
I am a farmer and also a State Senator.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.670
I represent district 32
Fillmore, Thayer, Jefferson,

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.930
Salem, and Southwestern Lancaster counties

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:21.660
here in Nebraska.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.890
The State of Nebraska's agriculture

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:28.140
is the fourth largest
ag-powerhouse in the nation.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:31.460
We feed way more people
with the food we produce

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.870
than just the 1.96 million people here

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:35.203
in the State of Nebraska.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.180
The farm that you're on
today is a farm that's common

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:41.120
to Southeast Nebraska.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.940
We rotate corn and soybeans.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:45.790
It takes hundreds of thousands of dollars

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.920
to operate a small farm like this.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.010
Our margins are razor thin.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:53.520
Farming is an industry where we buy retail

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:54.843
and we sell wholesale.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.520
While the principles of farming

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:58.780
remain the same,

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:02.493
the equipment used to farm
has changed dramatically.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.913
This is a 1952 Allis-Chalmers WD.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.070
This is the style of tractor I grew up on

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:14.070
in the '60s and '70s.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:19.070
This tractor has not
started for two months.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:31.680
It's tough, and it works.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.103
And this is what American farm
equipment used to be like.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:42.710
Farming equipment

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:45.470
for both John Deere and
their major competitors

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:48.730
has become much more
technologically sophisticated

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.126
in the past two decades.

00:06:51.126 --> 00:06:53.490
A little after 2000, tractors changed

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:56.920
from being strictly just a power source

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:59.373
and got to be a lot more computerized.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:03.040
Now they're all basically
drive-by-wire systems

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:04.710
where there's no physical connection

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:06.900
between any of our
controls in the tractor,

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.050
except a piece of wire.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.430
And every generation tractor since

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.500
is even more computerized.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.310
It surprises people every day

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:18.350
how technologically
sophisticated the equipment is

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:22.620
whether that's GNSS satellite
guidance in our equipment,

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.350
or whether it's the digital information

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:27.430
that comes off those equipment
that helps make customers...

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:29.780
enable them to make better
decisions every day.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.453
This is the main computer
screen right here.

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:42.440
It can show us moisture,
it can show us yield,

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:45.273
it can show us some various
components on the combine.

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.860
20 years ago, our irrigated corn

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:52.320
probably yielded 150,
60 bushels to the acre.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:53.970
This last crop we just harvested,

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:56.950
we did 250 bushels to the acre.

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:01.950
So if your crops are gaining
yield 40% over 10 or 20 years,

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:07.840
you're gonna need bigger,
faster equipment to harvest it.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:09.900
And those bigger, faster machines

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:14.570
have increased productivity
an estimated 1.4% per year

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.430
over the past 70 years,

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:19.370
and US farmers now produce
an average corn yield

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:22.720
of about 175 bushels an acre.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.440
And then the really valuable information

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.170
that starts to move markets is
what the harvest looks like.

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:34.170
So now one of these enormous
harvesters will tell,

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:37.310
in real time, through this modem,

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:39.790
under the farmer's rear end back to Deere

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:43.350
and who knows where,
to Goldman Sachs maybe?

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:47.020
Exactly how much corn or
wheat is being harvested

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:50.110
on that farm acreage at that moment.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:52.060
It's really staggering.

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:54.440
We get a lot more done in the day.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:56.120
We can work longer hours,

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:59.240
especially with the auto-steer,
auto-guidance systems.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:00.690
We can get a lot more hours in,

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:02.860
we're not tired at the end of the day.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:06.410
So the efficiency factor
is great when it works.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:08.810
Unfortunately, like all electronics,

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:09.963
it doesn't always work.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:16.490
And that's where there's a catch.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:19.640
While the technology in a
John Deere tractor or combine

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:21.980
makes farming easier than ever before,

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:25.050
there are very real
concerns from some farmers

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:27.020
when it comes to repairing that equipment

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:29.930
because as of right now, they can't.

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.730
John Deere and other
agricultural machinery companies

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.090
won't let farmers fix their own equipment

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:36.983
without a licensed mechanic.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.680
For Deere and its dealerships,

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:43.130
parts and services are three
to six times more profitable

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:45.020
than sales of original equipment

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:46.913
according to company filings.

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:52.460
Historically, farmers have always repaired

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:54.070
their own equipment.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:56.920
Farmers love to tinker, I will grant that.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:58.470
We all like to tinker with our equipment

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:00.740
and we all modify our equipment.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:03.610
The right to repair gets
down to the nuts and bolts

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:05.950
that currently, farmers don't have access

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:08.620
to the computer systems
on their farm equipment.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:10.730
It doesn't matter whether
it's green John Deere,

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:15.200
or it's red Case, or AGCO's,
they're all proprietary,

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.130
they won't let us in on that.

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:18.540
The thing that you fix on the tractor

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:21.760
isn't necessarily a moving part,

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:24.770
it may be something that has
to be fixed in the software

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:28.680
or hitting a reset
button for your computer.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:29.820
We've had a few cases

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:33.140
where we get an error code
on the panel and it is basic.

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:33.973
You look it up and it says,

00:10:33.973 --> 00:10:36.030
"Call your dealer."

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:37.820
So the dealer has to come out

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:40.220
and they plug their
computer into the tractor,

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.150
and they have to diagnose
what the problem is.

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:45.270
We have operators

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:48.550
who have had computer
glitches during planting.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:51.610
They have a newer tractor,
they have a newer planter.

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:53.060
It happens on a Friday,

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:55.370
they gotta wait till
Monday to get it fixed,

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:57.500
they've lost 2 1/2 days.

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:00.690
If they were given access
to the operating system,

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.160
they knew what they had
to do to repair that.

00:11:06.448 --> 00:11:07.700
And there are literally

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:10.480
billions of dollars at
stake for John Deere

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:12.350
when it comes to repairs.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:16.230
They make a lot of their
money in the dealerships

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:20.560
from parts and repair because
frankly, they have a monopoly,

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:24.690
and that monopoly has been
significantly enhanced

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:28.660
by these software controls
in the modern tractor.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:31.060
No one else can work on 'em.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:33.500
Annual parts sales increased 23%

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:37.700
to a $6.8 billion from 2013 to 2020,

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:40.840
while Deere's total agricultural
equipment sales fell

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.453
20% to $22.3 billion.

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:46.540
So there's very real money at stake

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:47.933
when it comes to repair.

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:50.660
If a right to repair law

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:53.520
allowed parts and services
markets to compete,

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:54.810
it could be a huge blow

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:57.920
to a vital income stream for the company.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:11:59.157
The farmers of the world say,

00:11:59.157 --> 00:12:01.820
"We've been repairing
these things forever."

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:03.910
Farmers know how to fix things,

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.590
know how to keep things
working in the field.

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:07.940
And by denying us that,

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:12.940
you're essentially taking
away a kind of birthright.

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.370
John Deere is also the target

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:18.180
of a potential federal
class-action lawsuit

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:22.970
alleging antitrust violations
tied to its repair policies.

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:25.740
John Deere declined to
comment on the litigation,

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:28.480
but said it meets company
commitments to provide parts,

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:32.193
information, and software
to owners of its equipment.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:39.230
However, there is precedent when it comes

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:40.970
to right to repair.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:42.810
The best way of explaining

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.880
the right to repair is to
go back to automobiles.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:47.970
As automobiles were built

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:51.720
with more and more software and chips,

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:56.720
it became more and more difficult
for the average car owner

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:00.040
to fix their car because of the software,

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:02.840
you didn't have the tools to do that.

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:04.750
So in 2012,

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:08.800
the State of Massachusetts
introduced a right to repair bill

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:10.830
on light cars and trucks.

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:13.290
They were the only state
in the nation to do that.

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:15.670
And what that did is that
opened the floodgates

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:19.880
and made it possible for
third-party mechanics,

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:24.880
the mom and pop, to access
and repair all those vehicles.

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:27.690
I can tell you locally,

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:29.560
we have a situation just like that.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:33.260
Last January of 2021,

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:36.290
based on the fact that
the Farm Bureau membership

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:38.950
overwhelmingly wanted to
see a right to repair bill,

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:40.630
we introduced one.

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:43.140
That bill called LB543

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:46.970
was voted in favor 176
to one by the farmers.

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:49.520
Yet right now, that bill sits in committee

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:52.070
because it required
amendments to only reference

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:54.820
repairing farm equipment
and not other electronics

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:57.690
like iPhones, computers and televisions.

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:59.330
Our position's fairly straightforward.

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:00.300
We believe that farmers

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:01.820
have the right to repair their equipment.

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:03.900
Where we draw the line
is the right to modify

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.820
and it generally involves
the embedded software

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:08.470
that we put on the machines.

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:12.200
Agricultural equipment is
less like consumer electronics

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:14.940
than it is really like aviation.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:16.480
You wouldn't want just anybody

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:19.110
fixing and repairing
software on an airplane

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:21.140
because there is a safety component to it

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:24.040
in the same way I think a
tractor going down the highway

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:26.380
is a fairly comparable situation.

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:29.590
That's really where our
efforts are best spent

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:32.250
to try to make sure that people
understand the ramifications

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:34.820
and the risks associated
with the conversations

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:36.575
that are currently happening in the space.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:42.000
While farmers are waiting

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.320
for a ruling to be made
on right to repair,

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:46.710
some are taking matters
into their own hands

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:48.830
by using gray market technology

00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:51.540
to run diagnostics on their own equipment.

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:55.700
When you rebel against
a technological barrier,

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:56.920
you hack it.

00:14:56.920 --> 00:14:59.470
And there are a number of farmers

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:03.320
who've availed themselves
of John Deere repair

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:07.220
and diagnostic software
from places like Hong Kong

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:10.720
and other markets where things are sold

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:14.620
without the proper licenses,
the software black market,

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:16.823
or in this case, it's
really a gray market.

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:20.870
Right now, the right to repair

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:22.203
is still moving forward.

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:28.180
But its future is anything but certain.

00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:32.270
Currently, LB543 is sitting in committee

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:36.050
and we chose to have it
stay there for one year

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:38.980
to allow all parties to
give us some input on this,

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:41.053
and it's pretty much been crickets.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:45.890
But the human cry out here in
the country is getting louder.

00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:48.630
People want more regulation

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:51.700
or at least the opportunity
to control their own destiny.

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:56.253
They don't like seeing it in
the hands of big corporations.

00:15:58.530 --> 00:15:59.470
We'll keep working at it

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:00.920
until we get it accomplished.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:03.933
I'm not afraid of a fight.

00:16:03.933 --> 00:16:06.730
I haven't met a farmer yet
that's not afraid of a fight.

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:10.623
So we'll state our peace and
see what we can get done.

